1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:28,230
Welcome to Update 20 of AHEAD Pathway. The Rehearsal room has grown. You can now bring your slides and your document into the room, and the panel reads them together with your evidence map, as one body of work. You will meet a panel you can see. And you will learn to answer first, and see yourself improve. Your reading, your thinking, your writing and your speaking, working together.

2
00:00:29,629 --> 00:01:11,769
First, the panel is now in the room with you. Three African scholars: the Chair, a senior professor; the Methodologist, who takes notes on everything you say; and the Specialist reader, who knows your literature. They speak in African voices: the Chair and the Methodologist in South African voices, the Specialist reader in a Kenyan voice. The member who asks speaks and gestures towards you, and the others turn to listen. While you answer, they nod and take notes. Before each question, they confer. It feels like the real room, because that is what you are preparing for.

3
00:01:13,170 --> 00:01:42,709
Before you enter the room, choose what you are presenting on: your proposal, a chapter, or any document on your record. Then attach your slides, a PowerPoint or a PDF. Your evidence map always comes too. The panel reads all of it together, slide by slide, and may ask you about a particular slide, just as a real panel would. Your slides are kept in Documents, as part of your body of work.

4
00:01:44,109 --> 00:02:13,210
Your debrief now opens with what the panel read, and then something powerful: where your slides, your document and your evidence map do not yet line up. A slide that promises more than your proposal plans. A contribution that rests on the thinnest part of your evidence. A lens at the heart of your proposal that no slide shows. Fix these, and your presentation, your writing and your reading all grow stronger together.

5
00:02:14,610 --> 00:02:31,080
When you answer aloud, the ring around the microphone now fills at two minutes, the length a real panel usually expects. The reminder is simple: say your answer in your first sentence, then give the reason, and one example from your study.

6
00:02:32,480 --> 00:02:53,780
Your debrief shows how you delivered your answers: the average length, how many led with the answer itself, whether you repeated yourself, and how this round compares with your last. Here, the average came down from four minutes to one and a half. That is what growth looks like, and now you can see it.

7
00:02:55,180 --> 00:03:12,460
When an answer was hard, the debrief shows which part of your study it belongs to, and how many readings your map holds there. Very often, the question you struggled with is exactly where your evidence is thinnest. One click opens that part of your map.

8
00:03:13,860 --> 00:03:32,689
Your map opens with that part highlighted. Add a reading of your own, or write a note on the readings already there, and next Monday, the readings chosen for you lean towards it. What you found hard to say becomes what you read next, and what you read makes the next answer stronger.

9
00:03:34,090 --> 00:03:55,930
Finally, after each debrief, tell us how useful the round was, from one to five, with a line if you would like. Your answer is kept without your name: the Director sees only the totals and the comments, never who wrote them. So please be candid: it is how the room keeps getting better for you.

10
00:03:57,330 --> 00:04:22,240
One body of work. A panel in the room. Answers that land. Read powerfully, think powerfully, write powerfully, and speak with confidence. Fifteen minutes in the Rehearsal room, once a week, and you will hear the difference. That is AHEAD Pathway: your degree, well supported, every step of the way.
